/E (MFI Crises in Context: The 2010 Andhra Pradesh crisis exposed the risks of unchecked expansion, where aggressive growth without repayment discipline led to borrower over- indebtedness, repayment fatigue, and reputational damage. Such episodes underscore the need to balance financial sustainability with social responsibility.)
/Pg 10 0 R
>>
endobj
123 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Repayment Incentives: High repayment rates are strongly influenced by reputational concerns, community trust, and the assurance of continued access to future credit cycles. For many borrowers, the opportunity to remain part of the program and secure subsequent loans is as valuable as the loan itself, reinforcing repayment discipline and long-term program loyalty.)
/Pg 10 0 R
>>
endobj
124 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Operational and Cost Challenges: MFIs face persistent cost pressures due to small loan sizes, geographically dispersed borrowers, and reliance on manual, cash-based repayment systems. These inefficiencies raise transaction costs, limit scalability, and constrain efforts to expand outreach into underserved communities.)
/Pg 10 0 R
>>
endobj
125 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (While institutions like Grameen Bank in Bangladesh pioneered social-collateral-based lending, Indian MFIs such as SKS Microfinance and Bandhan attempted rapid expansion but faced crises when repayment incentives eroded. Therefore, a sustainable model requires a balance between trust-based community mechanisms and modern financial technologies.)
/E (While institutions like Grameen Bank in Bangladesh pioneered social-collateral-based lending, Indian MFIs such as SKS Microfinance and Bandhan attempted rapid expansion but faced crises when repayment incentives eroded. Therefore, a sustainable model requires a balance between trust-based community mechanisms and modern financial technologies.)
/Pg 10 0 R
>>
endobj
